<section class="scenario-reveal">
  <h3 class="scenario-reveal__title">Try it: Interpret the Change</h3>
  <p class="scenario-reveal__prompt">Think through each scenario, then reveal a sample interpretation.</p>

  <details class="lesson-reveal">
    <summary>A plot's NDVI rises from 0.25 to 0.60 between two flights. What could this signify?</summary>
    <p>A rising NDVI generally signals improving plant health — the vegetation is reflecting more NIR light and less red light than before. This could mean the crop has grown further into a healthy growth stage, recovered from an earlier stress event (like a nutrient deficiency or water stress), or simply matured with fuller canopy coverage since the first flight.</p>
  </details>

  <details class="lesson-reveal">
    <summary>One plot has a very low NDVI value at both time points. What could this mean?</summary>
    <p>A consistently very low NDVI across both flights suggests that plot has little to no live vegetation present at all — most likely bare soil, a gap in the stand, or a spot where the crop failed to establish. Because the value stayed low rather than changing, it points to a persistent condition rather than a temporary stress event.</p>
  </details>
</section>

